"An activity diagram models token flow through actions, decisions, and concurrent paths in a behavior.\n\nUse when: Explain software workflow with alternatives and concurrency. Review object flow, control flow, and synchronization.\n\nAvoid when: Persistent entity states or exact message ordering is the question.\n\nVisual grammar: Rounded nodes encode actions; arrows carry control or object tokens. Diamonds encode decisions or merges with guards. Bars encode forks and joins; partitions optionally identify responsibility.\n\nBuild: Choose a trigger and explicit completion condition. Lay out the principal action path in one direction. Add guarded alternatives, concurrency, and relevant object flows. Use padded partitions and route exceptional paths around the main action labels.\n\nCheck: Decision guards are complete or explicitly allow an else path. Merges do not accidentally imply synchronization. Forked paths have intentional join behavior and cancellation semantics. Object labels, guard text, and partition titles do not intersect arrows.\n\nPitfalls: Using a join where a merge is required and creating an unintended wait. Confusing flow final with termination of the entire activity.\n\nExample: After validation, a booking activity reserves seats and authorizes payment concurrently, then joins before confirmation.\n\nAlternatives: State machine diagram, Petri net."
